    "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
    "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
    "senderName": "NWS Charleston WV",
    "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 20 at 3:44AM EDT until September 20 at 7:45AM EDT by NWS Charleston WV",
    "description": "FFWRLX\n\nThe National Weather Service in Charleston has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nNorthern Roane County in central West Virginia...\nCalhoun County in north central West Virginia...\nGilmer County in north central West Virginia...\nSouthwestern Doddridge County in northern West Virginia...\nNortheastern Jackson County in northwestern West Virginia...\nSouthern Ritchie County in northwestern West Virginia...\nWirt County in northwestern West Virginia...\nSoutheastern Wood County in northwestern West Virginia...\n\n* Until 745 AM EDT.\n\n* At 344 AM EDT, Doppler radar indicated thunderstorms producing\nheavy rain across the warned area. Between 1 and 1.5 inches of rain\nhave fallen. Additional rainfall amounts up to 1.5 inches are\npossible in the warned area. Flash flooding is ongoing or expected\nto begin shortly.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Radar indicated.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nSpencer, Glenville, Elizabeth, Grantsville, Arnoldsburg, Cedar\nCreek State Park, Reedy, Sand Fork, Pullman, Auburn, Munday,\nAnnamoriah, Brohard, Creston, Macfarlan, Big Springs, Mount Zion,\nSmithville, Cherry and Millstone.\n\nThis includes Interstate 77 in West Virginia near mile marker 150...\nand between mile markers 152 and 163.",
    "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nBe especially cautious at night when it is harder to recognize the\ndangers of flooding.",
    "response": "Avoid",
    "note": null,
